      "writtenOn": "2022-12-10T02:04:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Looking at both Dawn and Metal specs I think it\u0027s a little more complicated than that. Dawn always requires size and offsets to be aligned to 4 bytes, and Metal has the same limitation when on macOS. This is contrary to my assumption that they\u0027d behave like memcpy and function for unaligned source data to an aligned dest.\n\nHowever, we can\u0027t just align based on what we\u0027d end up doing with the draw buffer in all cases. For vertex data we\u0027re likely going to always be 4-byte aligned and a multiple of 4 for size because of using float data, but most/all index buffers that Skia uses are uint16\u0027s. As a data type those only require 2 byte alignment, so while we could always start the offset on a 4 byte boundary, we\u0027d need to pad and fill with zeros the range of data to 4 bytes as well.\n\nThis shouldn\u0027t be too hard to work into the logic as the transfer buffer hands out VertexWriters.",
      "parentUuid": "62168aa2_bd57b086",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 260,
        "startChar": 69,
        "endLine": 260,
        "endChar": 84
      },
      "revId": "2ca192605eb8a481226ed873825b7a546130c80a",
